1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,400
Fire changed how we survive.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Writing extended our memory.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:10,600
Machines amplified our strength.

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:15,000
And artificial intelligence is changing something deeper—

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:18,200
Thinking.

6
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:31,200
The machines of the past extended our hands.

7
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:36,000
Today's machines are starting to take part in creation, design and discovery.

8
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:41,800
Abilities once thought to be uniquely human

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,400
are being re-examined.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
As intelligence leaves the lab and enters everyone's life,

11
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:56,400
humanity's place is beginning to shift.

12
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:12,200
New jobs may emerge,

13
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,600
while old skills may quickly lose their value.

14
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:21,800
Harari worries that some people may struggle for a long time to find a place in the economy

15
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:25,600
— the risk of a "useless class."

16
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:31,600
No longer being needed for work never means a person has lost their worth.

17
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:35,000
Lifelong learning needs opportunity,

18
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,600
and the wealth technology creates must be shared.

19
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:44,800
As work changes, how will we rethink our own worth?

20
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,600
If machines can write poetry,

21
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:55,400
can paint,

22
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,600
can even help us discover new knowledge,

23
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,000
then is creativity still uniquely human?

24
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:12,200
Perhaps we need to rethink what creation means.

25
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,800
A song can carry one person's longing.

26
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:21,600
A painting can preserve the questions of an era.

27
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:27,000
Future creation will also happen between humans and new forms of intelligence.

28
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:32,600
And we must still find what we truly want to express.

29
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,800
Artificial intelligence may change the world.

30
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:48,000
But it cannot replace the questions humans ask.

31
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,200
Why do we exist?

32
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:56,600
What kind of future do we want to create?

33
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,600
As algorithms come to know us better and better,

34
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:04,800
we, too, must come to know ourselves anew.

35
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:09,000
What do we truly long for?

36
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:14,200
And for what kind of future are we willing to take responsibility?

37
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:17,800
Our true advantage as humans

38
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:21,000
was never being faster than machines—

39
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,200
it is having dreams,

40
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:27,000
having love,

41
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,000
having the power to choose the future.

42
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:35,800
The future is not yet written.

43
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:39,800
Our choices are becoming history.

